 > [!NOTE]
 > The header take precedence over the cookie.
 
+## Rotate the Password
+
+`password` also accepts a list. The first password seals new sessions, and the others are only tried when opening a session, in the order they are listed. A session sealed with an older password keeps working while that password is in the list, and is resealed with the first password the next time it is written (or, with `idleTimeout`, when its window slides).
+
+```js
+import { useSession } from "h3";
+
+app.use(async (event) => {
+  const session = await useSession(event, {
+    password: [
+      "new-80d42cfb-1cd2-462c-8f17-e3237d9027e9-with-32-characters",
+      "old-80d42cfb-1cd2-462c-8f17-e3237d9027e9-with-32-characters",
+    ],
+  });
+});
+```
+
+To rotate a password:
+
+1. Put the new password first and keep the old one after it.
+2. Wait until every session sealed with the old password has expired, which takes longer than the largest `maxAge` or `idleTimeout` you use.
+3. Remove the old password from the list.
+
+Each password that does not match costs a full PBKDF2 derivation, so opening a session sealed with the Nth password costs N - 1 extra derivations. The order of the list is also visible in response timing, so keep the list short and remove old passwords as soon as they are no longer needed.
+
 ## Get Data from a Session
 
 To get data from a session, we will still use `useSession`. Under the hood, it will use `getSession` to get the session.
